  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PWYP Coordinator Post</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outline of what work needs to be done, including details of the volume of work
Work is already of sufficient volume for a full-time Coordinator.
-Developing and implementing campaign plans with the PWYP Steering Committee and member agencies, e.g. follow up on the EU Transparency Obligations Directive.
-Ensuring members are updated with relevant information from various campaign initiatives, e.g. international accounting standards, Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative, lobbying of IFIs and Export Credit Agencies, progress on revenue transparency in individual countries.
-Developing briefings, for developing country members and others, on particular issues of relevance which may include international frameworks relating to financial transparency.
-Supporting and responding to developing country based members so that they are fully involved in the initiative. Organising events including training seminars in conjunction with other members of the campaign.
-Supporting member organisations in liaising with national governments and industry.
-Where appropriate, representing the campaign in meetings with government or industry
-Drafting campaign materials e.g. PWYP Newsletter, web copy, briefings, press releases and information materials.
-Maintaining updated email distribution lists and database of all coalition members.

PWYP shares CAFOD&apos;s values of solidarity and stewardship. The objective of the PWYP campaign is to ensure that the people of developing countries benefit from their natural resources.  To do this civil society needs to have information about the payments that companies make so that they can hold their governments to account for how those revenues are spent.  The PWYP coalition is an excellent example of a collaborative and joint campaigning initiative between Northern NGOs and Southern partners.  Since 2002 it has grown from 60 to 290 member organisations, many of which are in the South.  A major factor in this sustained growth has been the energy and effectiveness of the PWYP Coordinator.</narrative>
  </description>
